eth_transmit: send a frame only when it is completely in the FIFO.

The transmitter started a frame once 800 bytes were in the FIFO and then
needs the DMA to stay ahead of the wire (12.5 MB/s). Our DMA reads DDR2
through cpu_mem_bridge/mig_arb one word at a time, behind the CPU, at about
5 MB/s: every frame longer than about 1376 bytes ran the FIFO empty, the
RMII transmitter dropped the frame there (rmii_transceiver data0 without
valid) and sent the rest as fragments. TCP then lost every full-size
segment, retransmissions included (FTP RETR, large HTTP replies stalled).
The FIFO now holds 2048 bytes and the early start is at 1536 bytes, beyond
any Ethernet frame (1514), so frames go out store-and-forward whatever the
memory latency. designs/c64_basic/sim/eth/run.sh checks it.

--- a/fpga/io/rmii/vhdl_source/eth_transmit.vhd	2026-10-02 02:47:24
+++ b/fpga/io/rmii/vhdl_source/eth_transmit.vhd	2026-10-02 02:47:24
@@ -85,7 +85,7 @@
     generic map (
         --g_fast       => true,
         g_data_width => 9,
-        g_depth_bits => 10
+        g_depth_bits => 11  -- a whole frame (1514) fits: it goes out only when complete
     )
     port map (
         wr_clock     => clock,
@@ -253,7 +253,7 @@
                         wr_din(8) <= '0';
                     end if; 
     
-                    if up_count = 800 then -- this level has to be insanely high because of the insanely slow memory interface
+                    if up_count = 1536 then -- only a frame longer than any Ethernet frame starts before its end
                         trigger <= '1';
                         trigger_sent <= '1';
                     end if;
